<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-5-13-putting-it-together" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Putting It All Together: A Complete Case Study</title>

 <introduction>
    <sidebyside widths="70% 25%" valigns="middle top">
      <p>
        You now have all the tools you need to solve problems by decomposing them into sub-tasks implemented with methods. Let’s work through an example problem and then you can try some of the exercises at the end of the chapter.
      </p>
      <stack>
        <image source="figures/dice.jpg">
          <shortdescription>Two six-sided dice showing pips.</shortdescription>
        </image>
        <p>
          Two six-sided dice. <copyright /> Schlurcher, retrieved from <url href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spielw%C3%BCrfel.jpg" visual="commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spielw%C3%BCrfel.jpg">Wikimedia Commons</url>.
        </p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 5.13.1 The problem                                           -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-13-1-dice-game-problem">
    <title>The Problem</title>

    <p>
      Design a game for one user to play against the computer. The game will simulate the roll of a six-sided die. In the game, the players are trying to reach a threshold value by totaling die rolls. The game is over when one player reaches the threshold value (wins) or goes over the threshold (loses).
    </p>

    <p>
      The players take turns rolling the die. The value of the die is added to their total. The game is over when one of the following occurs:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><p>One player’s total equals the threshold (a win).</p></li>
      <li><p>One player’s total exceeds the threshold (a loss, called a bust).</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The game will proceed in rounds. Each round, the die will be rolled for the user and added to their total. The program will report the die roll. The program will print a message if the user has won or busted. If they haven’t, the die will be rolled for the computer and the roll will be reported. The program will print a message if the computer has won or busted. Both total scores will be reported, and if nobody has won or busted, the program proceeds to the next round.
    </p>

    <p>
      Note that you can enhance this game by giving both players the chance to hold<mdash />to stop rolling. This adds one more ending condition to the game: both players have chosen to hold. We will implement the simpler version of the game.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 5.13.2 Decomposing the problem                               -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-13-2-decomposing-dice-game">
    <title>Decomposing the Problem</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        To begin, we look for subtasks within the problem. While there are many ways to break a problem apart, we evaluate our proposed decomposition using the following criteria:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><strong>Single Responsibility:</strong> Each subtask focuses on one specific job that can be described in a simple sentence.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><strong>Reusability:</strong> The subtask eliminates duplicated code by packaging operations that occur multiple times (such as taking a turn for either the player or the computer).</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><strong>Information Hiding:</strong> The subtask hides low-level details or complex formulas so the higher-level program remains easy to read.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><strong>Well-Defined Contract:</strong> We can clearly define the subtask's interface: its inputs (parameters), its output (return value), and its pre- and postconditions.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><strong>No Reinventing the Wheel:</strong> The task is not already handled directly by an existing Java library method (such as <c>System.out.println</c> or <c>Math.max</c>).</p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Using these criteria, we will break the problem down into a <c>main</c> method and helper methods. <xref ref="figure-structure-chart-dice-game" /> is a structure chart depicting the program structure, including the data that will flow between these methods.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="figure-structure-chart-dice-game">
        <title>Structure Chart for the DiceGame Program</title>
        <caption>Functional decomposition of <c>main</c> into helper methods <c>rollDie</c>, <c>isGameOver</c>, and <c>displayOutcome</c>, showing data flows.</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_5_7.png" width="95%">
          <shortdescription>Structure chart showing main calling rollDie, isGameOver, and displayOutcome with arguments and return values.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              A hierarchical structure chart for DiceGame. The main method sits at the top as the caller. Three downward invocation arrows connect to subordinate helper methods: rollDie on the left, isGameOver in the center, and displayOutcome on the right. Data flow arrows annotate the connections: numSides (int) flows down to rollDie and dieRoll (int) flows up; total and threshold flow down to isGameOver and gameOver (boolean) flows up; and playerScore, computerScore, and threshold flow down to displayOutcome.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        From the structure chart, you can see that <c>main</c> calls <c>rollDie</c>, <c>isGameOver</c>, and <c>displayOutcome</c>. The algorithm within <c>main</c> (including where the methods are called) is:
      </p>

      <pre>
Print a welcome message
While the game isn't over:
    Take the user's turn (rollDie)
    If the user didn't win or bust (isGameOver)
        Take the computer's turn (rollDie)
        Check if the computer won or busted (isGameOver)
    Print the scores 
Announce the winner or loser (displayOutcome)
      </pre>

      <p>
        Let’s look at some of the design decisions made here and how they meet our evaluation criteria.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-5-13-2-1-separating-announcements">
      <title>Separating Announcing a Win or Loss from Determining if the Game is Over</title>

      <p>
        The <c>isGameOver</c> method is given a score and the threshold, and it determines if there’s a win or a bust. It could also print messages for these two states, either generically (e.g. <q>That's a bust!</q>), or if another parameter is passed identifying the player, it could provide a message like <c>displayOutcome</c> (<q>That's a win for you!</q> or <q>That's a win for the computer!</q>). However, if we designed <c>isGameOver</c> to perform these two tasks, then we would be violating our Single Responsibility principle.
      </p>

      <p>
        Separating the functionality into two separate methods gives each method a clear and simple contract with the rest of the program, making the method easier to write and test. It also makes the program easier to expand and modify. If we planned to enhance the game to allow the user or computer to hold at a particular total, we would need to modify <c>isGameOver</c> to consider this third condition (both players holding), and we would need to modify <c>displayOutcome</c> to look for a new win condition<mdash />both scores at or below the threshold and one score higher than the other. There is also now the potential for a tie. It will be much easier to make these modifications without the complexity of having to make both sets of changes in one method.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-5-13-2-2-general-rolldie">
      <title>Writing a General <c>rollDie</c> Method Rather than Two Separate Methods</title>

      <p>
        In the game, both the user and the computer roll the die, so it might be tempting to design two separate <c>rollDie</c> methods<mdash />one for the user and one for the computer. But if you were to implement both methods, you’d quickly discover that the methods are identical. Therefore only one method is needed, and can be called twice.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes there is code or functionality that is not quite identical. In those cases, you can factor the code into a single method. Any differences between the methods are added as parameters to specialize the method functionality.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-5-13-2-3-hiding-low-level-details">
      <title>Hiding Low-Level Details</title>

      <p>
        It might be tempting in <c>main</c> to generate the die roll directly inside it using a single line of arithmetic:
      </p>

      <program language="java">
<![CDATA[int roll = (int)(Math.random() * numSides) + 1;]]>
      </program>

      <p>
        Why did we bother extracting this single line of code into its own helper method, <c>rollDie</c>?
      </p>

      <p>
        We did so to practice information hiding. Even though the code is short, scaling a pseudo-random floating-point number into an integer range requires non-trivial mechanics: multiplying by <c>numSides</c>, applying operator precedence, casting to an integer, and shifting the result by one. Placing that expression inside <c>main</c> forces anyone reading the turn logic to mentally unpack that mathematical formula.
      </p>

      <p>
        Extracting that calculation into <c>rollDie(numSides)</c> offers three major software engineering benefits:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><strong>Intent-Revealing Code:</strong> The method call <c>rollDie(numSides)</c> immediately communicates what is happening in plain English, allowing <c>main</c> to stay focused on high-level turn progression rather than math mechanics.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><strong>Isolated Verification:</strong> We can equip <c>rollDie</c> with its own precondition assertion (<c>assert numSides &gt; 0</c>) and postcondition assertion (<c>assert 1 &lt;= dieRoll &amp;&amp; dieRoll &lt;= numSides</c>) to guarantee that the random number generation is strictly bounded before the rest of the game uses it.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><strong>Flexibility for Future Change:</strong> If we later decide to simulate rolling physical dice using a seeded <c>java.util.Random</c> generator or an external hardware sensor, we only have to modify the body of <c>rollDie</c>. The calling method, <c>main</c>, remains completely unchanged.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-5-13-2-4-avoiding-unnecessary-wrappers">
      <title>Knowing When Not to Decompose: Avoiding Unnecessary Wrappers</title>

      <p>
        Our final evaluation criterion is ensuring that we do not reinvent the wheel. Just as important as knowing when to create a method is knowing when to leave code in <c>main</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        In our game, we need to print a two-line welcome banner at the start, and we need to display the current scores at the end of each round:
      </p>

      <program language="java">
<![CDATA[System.out.println("Your score          ---> " + playerTotal + ".");
System.out.println("The computer's score ---> " + computerTotal + ".
");]]>
      </program>

      <p>
        We could have created a <c>printGreeting()</c> method and a <c>showScores()</c> method. However, doing so would add visual clutter without providing any real abstraction. Neither task involves calculations, neither hides complex decisions, and neither requires parameterized customization.
      </p>

      <p>
        Standard output statements using <c>System.out.println</c> are already simple, standard library tools. Wrapping a couple of straightforward print statements inside a custom helper method creates an unnecessary layer of indirection<mdash />it forces someone reading your program to jump down to a helper method body just to see a basic print call.
      </p>

      <p>
        Good software design is about balance: decompose code when it eliminates duplication, separates distinct responsibilities, or clarifies complex algorithms, but leave straightforward sequential statements right where they occur.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 5.13.3 Write method contracts and the testing plan           -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-13-3-method-contracts-testing">
    <title>Write Method Contracts and the Testing Plan</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Before implementing the solution, we precisely specify each method’s contract: its inputs, outputs, preconditions, and postconditions. This facilitates building each method as a component that can be smoothly integrated into the final system. We should also design a testing plan for each method so we can unit test our methods before we integrate.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-5-13-3-1-writing-method-contract">
      <title>Writing a Method Contract</title>

      <p>
        The contracts for each method in our program are documented in the Javadoc comment above each method in the solution (<xref ref="listing-dice-game-solution" />), but we’ll discuss one method in detail: <c>rollDie</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        From the structure chart, you can see that <c>rollDie</c> takes one input: the number of sides of the die being rolled (an <c>int</c>). It randomly generates and returns a roll between one and the number of sides (inclusive).
      </p>

      <p>
        What is the contract between <c>rollDie</c> and its calling method? As a precondition, we specify <c>numSides &gt;= 1</c>. How did we determine that? First, from a domain perspective, a die represents a physical object<mdash />a die with zero or negative sides does not exist in reality. (We might argue that a die with one side, which would always roll a one, also doesn’t exist! We decided to allow it because the method can return a meaningful result with that input.) From an algorithmic perspective, our scaling formula <c>(int)(Math.random() * numSides) + 1</c> mathematically assumes a positive scaling factor. Passing a negative number breaks the uniform distribution due to integer truncation.
      </p>

      <p>
        The precondition <c>numSides &gt;= 1</c> protects both the integrity of our game's rules and the mathematics of our random generation.
      </p>

      <p>
        Given that precondition, our postcondition is that the value returned will be between <m>1</m> and <c>numSides</c> inclusive.
      </p>

      <p>
        To check the precondition, we write an <c>assert</c> statement before we generate the random roll value:
      </p>

      <program language="java">
<![CDATA[assert numSides > 0 : "rollDie called with d" + numSides;]]>
      </program>

      <p>
        We can also check the postcondition at the end of the method:
      </p>

      <program language="java">
<![CDATA[assert 1 <= dieRoll && dieRoll <= numSides : "Randomly generated value out of range";]]>
      </program>

      <p>
        This ensures that our algorithm is never generating a value outside of the expected range, which would indicate a problem with the method’s code.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-5-13-3-2-method-testing-plan">
      <title>Writing a Method’s Testing Plan</title>

      <p>
        To test <c>rollDie</c>, we divide the input domain into two equivalence classes: an invalid partition (<c>numSides &lt;= 0</c>) where the method's precondition assertion should trip, and a valid partition (<c>numSides &gt;= 1</c>) where the method should return a random integer between <m>1</m> and <c>numSides</c>. To trip the assertion, we can use the boundary value of <c>0</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        For valid inputs, however, we cannot predict the exact value returned by any single call because the result is pseudo-random. So how do we test a method that generates a random number? We want to ensure two things:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><p>The method never generates a value outside of the postcondition range.</p></li>
        <li><p>The method will generate all of the numbers within the range.</p></li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        To ensure that both conditions are met, we can write a test method that invokes <c>rollDie</c> in a loop with flags for each value in the expected range (e.g. 1<ndash />6 for a <c>numSides</c> value of 6) and a check that no value is out of the range. If each value is seen at least once and no value is ever generated outside of the range, we know our postcondition is met. The loop must iterate enough times to ensure that each number has a high probability of being generated. For a range of 1<ndash />6, a loop that iterates 1,000 times is an excellent choice because the loop will run in less than a millisecond, and because there is a less than one-in-a-trillion chance that one of the six numbers won’t be generated if the code is correct. For a much larger <c>numSides</c> value, such as 100, you should run the loop about 2,000<fn>Technically, <m>1{,}842</m> rolls for a one-in-a-million chance of failing on correct code.</fn> times to achieve the same high probability.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now that we know how to test that the method is generating all of the values within a range (and only the values within a range), we should choose values for the range. A good typical value to test is 6. The lower bound is 1, so we should test that value. We might also test an input of 2, which is the lowest bound for which more than one value is generated. For an upper bound, we could test 20<mdash />a typical number of sides for a die in tabletop role-playing games, or we could choose to test 100 (another typical die size), but for 100, we should increase the number of iterations. The test plan for <c>rollDie</c> is shown in <xref ref="table-test-plan-roll-die" />. A unit testing program for test case 4 is shown in <xref ref="listing-test-roll-die" />.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-test-plan-roll-die">
        <title>Test plan for unit testing <c>rollDie</c></title>
        <tabular>
          <col width="10%" />
          <col width="14%" />
          <col width="38%" />
          <col width="38%" />
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Test Case</cell>
            <cell><c>numSides</c></cell>
            <cell>Purpose / Equivalence Class</cell>
            <cell>Expected Behavior</cell>
          </row>
          <row top="small">
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>0</cell>
            <cell>Invalid boundary (below minimum)</cell>
            <cell>Precondition assert trips</cell>
          </row>
          <row top="small">
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>Valid lower boundary (degenerate die)</cell>
            <cell>Deterministic: always returns 1</cell>
          </row>
          <row top="small">
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>Minimal non-trivial die (coin flip)</cell>
            <cell>Returns {1, 2}; both appear</cell>
          </row>
          <row top="small">
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>Nominal game case (d6)</cell>
            <cell>Returns {1..6}; all appear in 1,000 rolls</cell>
          </row>
          <row top="small">
            <cell>5</cell>
            <cell>20</cell>
            <cell>Realistic high bound (d20)</cell>
            <cell>Returns {1..20}; all appear in 1,000 rolls</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <listing xml:id="listing-test-roll-die">
        <caption>A unit test for rollDie with numSides = 6</caption>
        <program language="java">
<![CDATA[/**
 * Unit test driver for DiceGame.rollDie(6).
 * Verifies that a 6-sided die never generates out-of-bounds values
 * and that all six faces are reachable over 1,000 rolls.
 */
public class TestRollDie {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Running unit test for rollDie(6)...
");

        final int SIDES = 6;
        final int ROLLS = 1000;

        boolean seen1 = false;
        boolean seen2 = false;
        boolean seen3 = false;
        boolean seen4 = false;
        boolean seen5 = false;
        boolean seen6 = false;

        int outOfBoundsCount = 0;

        // Roll the die 1,000 times
        for (int i = 0; i < ROLLS; i++) {
            int roll = DiceGame.rollDie(SIDES);

            // 1. Boundary check: ensure no roll falls outside [1, 6]
            if (roll < 1 || roll > SIDES) {
                outOfBoundsCount++;
                System.out.println("FAIL: Out-of-bounds roll generated: " + roll);
            }

            // 2. Reachability tracking: record which numbers appear
            if (roll == 1) {
                seen1 = true;
            } else if (roll == 2) {
                seen2 = true;
            } else if (roll == 3) {
                seen3 = true;
            } else if (roll == 4) {
                seen4 = true;
            } else if (roll == 5) {
                seen5 = true;
            } else if (roll == 6) {
                seen6 = true;
            } // if
        } // for

        // Evaluate whether every face appeared at least once
        boolean allFacesSeen = seen1 && seen2 && seen3 && seen4 && seen5 && seen6;

        // Display results breakdown
        System.out.println("Results for " + ROLLS + " rolls of a d" + SIDES + ":");
        System.out.println("  Face 1 appeared: " + seen1);
        System.out.println("  Face 2 appeared: " + seen2);
        System.out.println("  Face 3 appeared: " + seen3);
        System.out.println("  Face 4 appeared: " + seen4);
        System.out.println("  Face 5 appeared: " + seen5);
        System.out.println("  Face 6 appeared: " + seen6);

        // Final verdict
        System.out.println();
        if (outOfBoundsCount == 0 && allFacesSeen) {
            System.out.print("ALL TESTS PASSED: rollDie(6) is strictly bounded ");
            System.out.println("and all faces are reachable.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("TEST RUN FAILED.");
            if (outOfBoundsCount > 0) {
                System.out.println("  " + outOfBoundsCount + " roll(s) were out of bounds.");
            }
            if (!allFacesSeen) {
                System.out.println("  One or more die faces were never rolled.");
            }
        }
    }
}]]>
        </program>
      </listing>

      <p>
        The loop that is testing <c>rollDie</c> is on lines 24<ndash />47. In this loop, the method is invoked. The first check, on line 28, tests that the result is within bounds. The series <c>if</c> block from lines 34<ndash />45 then sets the one-way flags for each of the values that can be rolled. These flags start <c>false</c> (the value has not been rolled), and are flipped to <c>true</c> once the value is seen. They are one-way flags, meaning they’re never flipped back to <c>false</c>. We are only concerned with recording whether each value is seen at least once. The rest of the code after the loop reports the results of the reachability tracking.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 5.13.4 Implementing the solution                             -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-13-4-implementing-dice-game">
    <title>Implementing the Solution</title>

    <p>
      Once each method's contract and test plan are established, the methods can be individually implemented and unit-tested. In parallel, the <c>DiceGame</c> class and its <c>main</c> method can be written, using stubs in place of the helper methods until each one is ready for integration. On a professional software engineering team, different engineers might implement different helper methods simultaneously; shared method contracts ensure that all the components fit together seamlessly during integration.
    </p>

    <p>
      A <term>method stub</term> consists of a complete method header followed by a minimal body: an empty block for a <c>void</c> method, or a single statement returning a dummy value for a value-returning method. For example, a stub for <c>rollDie</c> can be written as:
    </p>

    <program language="java">
<![CDATA[public static int rollDie(int numSides) {
    return 1; // Temporary dummy return value
}]]>
    </program>

    <p>
      Stubs allow <c>main</c> to compile and execute immediately. In fact, if you pair this <c>rollDie</c> stub with a basic <c>isGameOver</c> stub and an empty <c>displayOutcome</c> stub, you can run a complete, deterministic simulation of the game: both players will roll 1 on every turn, and the game loop will reliably terminate after exactly 15 rounds. This allows you to verify the overall control flow in <c>main</c> before writing a single line of random number generation.
    </p>

    <p>
      Once each helper method is fully implemented and passes its unit tests, we swap out the stubs for the real methods. <xref ref="listing-dice-game-solution" /> displays the complete, fully integrated <c>DiceGame</c> program.
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing-dice-game-solution">
      <caption>A solution to the die-rolling game problem</caption>
      <program language="java">
<![CDATA[/**
 * Plays a dice game against the user in which dice are rolled until a threshold is
 * reached (a win) or passed (a loss).
 */
public class DiceGame {

    /**
     * Plays the dice-rolling game by repeatedly rolling the dice for the player and
     * computer until either wins or loses.
     *
     * @param args Not used.
     */
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        final int THRESHOLD = 15;    // The threshold that ends the game.
        final int SIDES = 6;         // The number of sides on the die.

        int playerTotal = 0;         // The player's die total.
        int computerTotal = 0;       // The computer's die total.
        boolean gameOver = false;    // Set to true when a player wins or loses.
        int roll;                    // The value of the current roll.

        // Print the instructions.
        System.out.println("Try to reach " + THRESHOLD + " without going over.");
        System.out.println("Good luck!
");

        // Play rounds until the game is over.
        while (!gameOver) {
            // Give the user a turn and determine if they won or busted.
            roll = rollDie(SIDES);
            System.out.println("You rolled " + roll + ".");
            playerTotal += roll;
            gameOver = isGameOver(playerTotal, THRESHOLD);

            // If the game isn't over, give the computer a turn;
            // determine if they won or busted.
            if (!gameOver) {
                roll = rollDie(SIDES);
                System.out.println("The computer rolled " + roll + ".");
                computerTotal += roll;
                gameOver = isGameOver(computerTotal, THRESHOLD);
            }

            // Show the current scores for both players.
            System.out.println("Your score          ---> " + playerTotal + ".");
            System.out.println("The computer's score ---> " + computerTotal + ".
");

        } // while

        // The game is over; announce the winner or loser.
        displayOutcome(playerTotal, computerTotal, THRESHOLD);
    }

    /**
     * Simulates the roll of a single die by randomly generating a
     * value between 1 and numSides.
     *
     * Precondition:  numSides >= 1
     * Postcondition: Returns a value between 1 and numSides.
     *
     * @param numSides  The number of sides of the die.
     * @return          A randomly generated value between 1 and numSides.
     */
    public static int rollDie(int numSides) {
        assert numSides > 0 : "rollDie called with d" + numSides;
        int dieRoll = (int)(Math.random() * numSides) + 1;
        assert 1 <= dieRoll && dieRoll <= numSides : 
              "Randomly generated value out of range";
        return dieRoll;
    }

    /**
     * Determines whether a player's score has ended the game by reaching
     * or exceeding the winning threshold.
     *
     * Precondition:  total >= 0 && threshold > 0
     * Postcondition: Returns true if total >= threshold; false otherwise.
     *
     * @param total     the player's current total score
     * @param threshold the target score required to win
     * @return          true if the score meets or exceeds the threshold; false otherwise
     */
    public static boolean isGameOver(int total, int threshold) {
        assert total >= 0 && threshold > 0 : 
               "Invalid score or threshold: " + total + ", " + threshold;
        return total >= threshold;
    }

    /**
     * Displays the final game outcome, announcing whether the player or 
     * the computer won (by matching the threshold) or busted (by exceeding it).
     *
     * Precondition:  playerScore >= 0 && computerScore >= 0 && threshold > 0,
     *                and at least one player's score must be >= threshold.
     * Postcondition: Displays a win announcement if a score equals threshold,
     *                or a bust announcement if a score exceeds threshold.
     *
     * @param playerScore   the player's final total score
     * @param computerScore the computer's final total score
     * @param threshold     the target score required to win
     */
    public static void displayOutcome(int playerScore, int computerScore, int threshold) {
        assert playerScore >= 0 && computerScore >= 0 && threshold > 0 : 
            "Invalid score or threshold values: " + playerScore 
            + ", " + computerScore + ", " + threshold;
        assert (playerScore >= threshold || computerScore >= threshold) : 
            "displayOutcome called before any player reached the threshold";

        System.out.println();
        if (playerScore == threshold) {
            System.out.println("---> That's a win for you! <---");
        } else if (playerScore > threshold) {
            System.out.println("---> That's a bust for you! <---");
        } else if (computerScore == threshold) {
            System.out.println("---> That's a win for the computer! <---");
        } else {
            System.out.println("---> That's a bust for the computer! <---");
        }
    }
}]]>
      </program>
    </listing>
  </subsection>

</section>
